  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds in from the west through \nmost of this week with sunny skies. Building hts as upper ridge\nstrengthens sends temps soaring through the 80s and nearing 90\nfor some interior locales (record high climate section below). \nA back door front moves into nrn parts of ENC later Thur with\nsome increasing cloud cover and slightly cooler temps behind \nit, but quickly retreats north by early Friday with another \nsurge of swrly flow ahead of much stronger front. No moisture \nwith the backdoor front, and only temporary reductions in temps \nfor nrn 1/3 of ENC. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The aforementioned much stronger cold front will\nsweep through ENC Friday night, and good model agreement with\nthis system. Front still appears moisture stared due to it's\nvery fast movement and no connection to the Gulf. Strong forcing\nis associated with it however, so a skinny line of showers may\ndrop only a few hundredths at best for most areas in the span of\nless than an hour Fri evening. Coolest and driest airmass \nfor the fall season thus far builds in behind this front with\ntemps taking a nosedive with highs in the 60s and lows in the\n40s (inland) and 50s (beaches). \n\nWith this strong front, winds rapidly inc from the NW Fri \nevening with a short period of gales for most of the waters and \nsounds, and would bring a sharp increase in high seas especially\non Sat as the winds turn northerly and persist to near gales on\nthe coastal waters. Model consistency and high ensemble probs\nresult in an increasing confidence on the stronger winds and\nhigher seas, although exact timing and strength is still unclear\nwith minimal hi-res guidance available during the time period. Minor\ncoastal impacts could occur too, though short duration of \nstrongest winds may act to limit more appreciable impacts. It's \nstill too soon for specifics on coastal flooding potential.",
